theory.intro:
To solve $mx+c=k$ from a graph, use the line $y=mx+c$. The solution is the $x$-value of the point where the line reaches the height $y=k$.

[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[every node/.style={font=\large}]
\usetikzlibrary{arrows.meta}
\draw[-{Stealth}] (-0.9,0) -- (4.4,0) node[right] {$x$};
\draw[-{Stealth}] (0,-1.5) -- (0,3.0) node[above] {$y$};
\draw[thick,blue!70!black,<->] (-0.6,-1.28) -- (3.8,2.24);
\node[right] at (3.95,2.24) {$y=mx+c$};
\draw[thick,red!75!black,dashed] (0,1.6) -- (3.8,1.6);
\node[right] at (3.95,1.6) {$y=k$};
\node[left] at (0,1.6) {$k$};
\draw[dashed] (3,1.6) -- (3,0);
\fill (3,1.6) circle (2.2pt);
\node[below] at (3,-0.1) {solution};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

facts:
To solve $mx+c=0$, read the **$x$-intercept**, where the line crosses the $x$-axis — the horizontal line $y=0$.

[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[every node/.style={font=\large}]
\usetikzlibrary{arrows.meta}
\draw[-{Stealth}] (-0.9,0) -- (4.4,0) node[right] {$x$};
\draw[-{Stealth}] (0,-1.5) -- (0,3.0) node[above] {$y$};
\draw[thick,blue!70!black,<->] (-0.6,-1.28) -- (3.8,2.24);
\node[right] at (3.95,2.24) {$y=mx+c$};
\node[above] at (3.4,0) {$y=0$};
\fill (1,0) circle (2.2pt);
\node[below right] at (1,0) {solution};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

workedExamples.question_text:
Use the graph of $y=x-2$ to solve $x-2=-4$.

[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}
\definecolor{graphblue}{HTML}{268CFF}
\begin{axis}[width=7cm,height=7cm,axis lines=middle,xmin=-4,xmax=4,ymin=-6,ymax=3,xtick={-4,-3,...,4},ytick={-6,-5,...,3},grid=major,grid style={draw=black!20},axis line style={black},tick style={black},ticklabel style={font=\fontsize{8.5}{10}\selectfont,text=black},label style={font=\fontsize{10}{12}\selectfont,text=black},xlabel={$x$},ylabel={$y$}]
\addplot[graphblue,thick,<->,domain=-4:4,samples=2]{x-2};
\end{axis}
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

workedExamples.solution_text:
Draw the horizontal line $y=-4$. It meets $y=x-2$ at $(-2,-4)$, so $x=-2$.

[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}
\definecolor{graphblue}{HTML}{268CFF}
\definecolor{targetred}{HTML}{EF6068}
\begin{axis}[width=7cm,height=7cm,axis lines=middle,xmin=-4,xmax=4,ymin=-6,ymax=3,xtick={-4,-3,...,4},ytick={-6,-5,...,3},grid=major,grid style={draw=black!20},axis line style={black},tick style={black},ticklabel style={font=\fontsize{8.5}{10}\selectfont,text=black},label style={font=\fontsize{10}{12}\selectfont,text=black},xlabel={$x$},ylabel={$y$}]
\addplot[graphblue,thick,<->,domain=-4:4,samples=2]{x-2};
\addplot[targetred,thick,dashed,domain=-4:4,samples=2]{-4};
\draw[black,dashed] (axis cs:-2,-4)--(axis cs:-2,0);
\fill[black] (axis cs:-2,-4) circle (2pt);
\node[anchor=north west,text=black,font=\fontsize{10}{12}\selectfont] at (axis cs:-2,-4) {$(-2,-4)$};
\node[anchor=south east,text=targetred,font=\fontsize{10}{12}\selectfont] at (axis cs:4,-4) {$y=-4$};
\end{axis}
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

workedExamples.question_text:
Use the graph of $y=4-x$ to solve $4-x=0$.

[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}
\definecolor{graphblue}{HTML}{268CFF}
\begin{axis}[width=7cm,height=7cm,axis lines=middle,xmin=-1,xmax=6,ymin=-2,ymax=5,xtick={-1,0,...,6},ytick={-2,-1,...,5},grid=major,grid style={draw=black!20},axis line style={black},tick style={black},ticklabel style={font=\fontsize{8.5}{10}\selectfont,text=black},label style={font=\fontsize{10}{12}\selectfont,text=black},xlabel={$x$},ylabel={$y$}]
\addplot[graphblue,thick,<->,domain=-1:6,samples=2]{4-x};
\end{axis}
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

workedExamples.solution_text:
$y=0$ is the $x$-axis. The graph crosses it at $(4,0)$, so $x=4$.

[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}
\definecolor{graphblue}{HTML}{268CFF}
\begin{axis}[width=7cm,height=7cm,axis lines=middle,xmin=-1,xmax=6,ymin=-2,ymax=5,xtick={-1,0,...,6},ytick={-2,-1,...,5},grid=major,grid style={draw=black!20},axis line style={black},tick style={black},ticklabel style={font=\fontsize{8.5}{10}\selectfont,text=black},label style={font=\fontsize{10}{12}\selectfont,text=black},xlabel={$x$},ylabel={$y$}]
\addplot[graphblue,thick,<->,domain=-1:6,samples=2]{4-x};
\fill[black] (axis cs:4,0) circle (2pt);
\node[anchor=south west,text=black,font=\fontsize{10}{12}\selectfont] at (axis cs:4,0) {$(4,0)$};
\end{axis}
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

workedExamples.question_text:
Use the graph of $y=6-2x$ to solve $6-2x=1$.

[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}
\definecolor{graphblue}{HTML}{268CFF}
\begin{axis}[width=7cm,height=7cm,axis lines=middle,xmin=-1,xmax=5,ymin=-4,ymax=8,xtick={-1,0,...,5},ytick={-4,-2,...,8},minor y tick num=1,grid=both,grid style={draw=black!20},axis line style={black},tick style={black},ticklabel style={font=\fontsize{8.5}{10}\selectfont,text=black},label style={font=\fontsize{10}{12}\selectfont,text=black},xlabel={$x$},ylabel={$y$}]
\addplot[graphblue,thick,<->,domain=-1:5,samples=2]{6-2*x};
\end{axis}
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

workedExamples.solution_text:
The labelled $y$-ticks increase by $2$; each small grid interval is $1$. Draw $y=1$, halfway between $y=0$ and $y=2$.

The intersection is halfway between $x=2$ and $x=3$, at $(2.5,1)$, so $x=2.5$.

[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}
\definecolor{graphblue}{HTML}{268CFF}
\definecolor{targetred}{HTML}{EF6068}
\begin{axis}[width=7cm,height=7cm,axis lines=middle,xmin=-1,xmax=5,ymin=-4,ymax=8,xtick={-1,0,...,5},ytick={-4,-2,...,8},minor y tick num=1,grid=both,grid style={draw=black!20},axis line style={black},tick style={black},ticklabel style={font=\fontsize{8.5}{10}\selectfont,text=black},label style={font=\fontsize{10}{12}\selectfont,text=black},xlabel={$x$},ylabel={$y$}]
\addplot[graphblue,thick,<->,domain=-1:5,samples=2]{6-2*x};
\addplot[targetred,thick,dashed,domain=-1:5,samples=2]{1};
\draw[black,dashed] (axis cs:2.5,1)--(axis cs:2.5,0);
\fill[black] (axis cs:2.5,1) circle (2pt);
\node[anchor=south west,text=black,font=\fontsize{10}{12}\selectfont] at (axis cs:2.5,1) {$(2.5,1)$};
\node[anchor=south east,text=targetred,font=\fontsize{10}{12}\selectfont] at (axis cs:5,1) {$y=1$};
\end{axis}
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]
